    {
      "id": "b-71",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Mission"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-72",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Change/Add:** Add mission, vision, and theory of change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-73",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mission: NewRoot improves the experiences of young people by increasing the capacity of adults to grow restorative and social-emotional practices in learning environments."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-74",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Theory of Change"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-75",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Notes:** Ryan to upload updated Theory of Change with 4 long term outcomes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-76",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Values + Norms"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-77",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Change/Add:** Add Norms:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-78",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "NewRoot Internal Norms"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-79",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "(Accepted 1.25.24)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-80",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Choose to participate. One of the most powerful tools we have is our attention and engagement. In order to meet our mission we must all choose to participate in the sessions, meetings, conversations, and activities that support our work. Participation looks like sharing our thoughts, feelings, beliefs, disagreements, concerns, trepidations, etc as we have capacity to do so. Because we know that personal experience can vary greatly depending on social identities (gender, race, lineage, sexuality, family-structure, income, etc), we will trust that each of us is offering the participation within our capacity. This means understanding there is no “one way to participate” but that participation will look different for each of us throughout time but we’re all committed to giving what we can, when we can, to further our mission together. (Values: Community and Curiosity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-81",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Speak your truth. Meet differences with curiosity and respect rather than defensiveness and reactivity. We each need to contribute our unique insight and truth as we move towards our mission. In order to speak our truth we must be courageous and vulnerable in our honest sharing of what we see, hear, feel, want, and need for our work. With that, we also know that there will be multiple truths present in our conversations and that can be challenging. We must be able to hold nuance and contradiction with empathy and an understanding of our own triggers and biases so as not to project those triggers onto another who disagrees with us. (Values: Curiosity, Dignity, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-82",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Stories stay, lessons leave. We aim to cultivate spaces that welcome vulnerability and engender trust. Doing so requires us to maintain confidentiality, especially when it comes to sensitive information. Sensitive information may include experiences (such as anecdotes from partner sites, details from someone’s personal life, or information about past traumas or hardships). Keep in mind that many social identities, (including but not limited to: citizenship status, LGBTQ+ identities, disability status, or spiritual beliefs), in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-83",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and of themselves may be sensitive information and should not be disclosed without explicit permission to do so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-84",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We talk to each other, not about each other. If a person shares something with you or a larger group that sat poorly with you or requires clarification, we encourage you to bring a conversation to that person directly, rather than talk behind their back. (Values: Dignity, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-85",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pause. Take a minute to check in with yourself. We believe not all discomfort is productive; you do not always have to step into conflict. If you are feeling overwhelmed, take a minute to ground yourself. Use the Check Yourself tool to bring awareness to how you are showing up mentally, physically, and emotionally. Recognize and ask for what you need."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-86",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We’re committed to taking a pause when we need to and respecting when a pause is necessary for others, even if it feels at an inopportune moment. Restorative processing takes time, and we encourage you to question and resist internal drives to rush through resolutions or sweep conflict & discomfort under the rug. (Values: Radical Love, Dignity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-87",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Trust the process and process the distrust. We recognize that trust is imperative to our work. We are a community comprised of individuals from many backgrounds. We understand and relate to things from unique perspectives and experiences. Trust that our team serves the same mission and trust in the restorative process."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-88",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Growth happens in the discomfort. Sometimes productive, meaningful conversations are uncomfortable. Just because something is uncomfortable does not mean it is unhealthy or unsafe. We aim to hold space for negative feelings and confusion. Instead of ignoring these feelings, we encourage you instead to ask questions and voice your concerns. We build trust through honesty, patience, and open communication. (Values: Community, Radical Love, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-89",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pay attention to patterns of participation and power dynamics in this space. We recognize that there are many factors that impact interaction. Remain attentive to patterns of participation in the community and your participation within the collective dynamic. Acknowledge and name who is speaking (role within organization, personal relationships, social identities, race, financial context, ability, etc) as well as who the audience is. Don’t wait for the facilitator."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-90",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Be aware of the context in which an interaction is occurring (place, audience, time, etc). Be aware of yourself and self-monitor the frequency and content of your contributions. This is a community responsibility. (Values: Community, relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-91",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Acknowledge and be accountable for your impact. We recognize that it is not our place to determine whether or not we have caused harm, only to own the impact that we have had. Harm happens on a spectrum. Be active and courageous in the restorative process–if you feel that you have harmed, or someone has communicated that you caused harm, take steps to repair it. Repairing harm can be a collaborative process and can include managers and other team members when support is needed. (Values: community, dignity, relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-92",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Take steps to communicate harm or agree to move past the conflict accepting it as part of your own work (72 hr.): We recognize that in order to further accept and normalize conflict as part of transformative work, we need to reflect, confront, and heal. This work will trigger emotions (such as pain or confusion and social power dynamics). Once harm is realized, go directly to the person who may have harmed you or whom you are concerned you may have harmed looking to gain clarity as well as move forward."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-93",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This does not mean reaching resolution in 72 hrs. This means taking a step towards reflection, conversation or healing. This will look different in every situation and to every individual. There is no one way to move forward. (Values: relationships, dignity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-94",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Notes:** New Page"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-95",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Change/Add:** Add Norms:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-96",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "NewRoot Internal Norms"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-97",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "(Accepted 1.25.24)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-98",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Choose to participate. One of the most powerful tools we have is our attention and engagement. In order to meet our mission we must all choose to participate in the sessions, meetings, conversations, and activities that support our work. Participation looks like sharing our thoughts, feelings, beliefs, disagreements, concerns, trepidations, etc as we have capacity to do so. Because we know that personal experience can vary greatly depending on social identities (gender, race, lineage, sexuality, family-structure, income, etc), we will trust that each of us is offering the participation within our capacity. This means understanding there is no “one way to participate” but that participation will look different for each of us throughout time but we’re all committed to giving what we can, when we can, to further our mission together. (Values: Community and Curiosity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-99",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Speak your truth. Meet differences with curiosity and respect rather than defensiveness and reactivity. We each need to contribute our unique insight and truth as we move towards our mission. In order to speak our truth we must be courageous and vulnerable in our honest sharing of what we see, hear, feel, want, and need for our work. With that, we also know that there will be multiple truths present in our conversations and that can be challenging. We must be able to hold nuance and contradiction with empathy and an understanding of our own triggers and biases so as not to project those triggers onto another who disagrees with us. (Values: Curiosity, Dignity, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-100",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Stories stay, lessons leave. We aim to cultivate spaces that welcome vulnerability and engender trust. Doing so requires us to maintain confidentiality, especially when it comes to sensitive information. Sensitive information may include experiences (such as anecdotes from partner sites, details from someone’s personal life, or information about past traumas or hardships). Keep in mind that many social identities, (including but not limited to: citizenship status, LGBTQ+ identities, disability status, or spiritual beliefs), in"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-101",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "and of themselves may be sensitive information and should not be disclosed without explicit permission to do so."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-102",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We talk to each other, not about each other. If a person shares something with you or a larger group that sat poorly with you or requires clarification, we encourage you to bring a conversation to that person directly, rather than talk behind their back. (Values: Dignity, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-103",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pause. Take a minute to check in with yourself. We believe not all discomfort is productive; you do not always have to step into conflict. If you are feeling overwhelmed, take a minute to ground yourself. Use the Check Yourself tool to bring awareness to how you are showing up mentally, physically, and emotionally. Recognize and ask for what you need."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-104",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We’re committed to taking a pause when we need to and respecting when a pause is necessary for others, even if it feels at an inopportune moment. Restorative processing takes time, and we encourage you to question and resist internal drives to rush through resolutions or sweep conflict & discomfort under the rug. (Values: Radical Love, Dignity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-105",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Trust the process and process the distrust. We recognize that trust is imperative to our work. We are a community comprised of individuals from many backgrounds. We understand and relate to things from unique perspectives and experiences. Trust that our team serves the same mission and trust in the restorative process."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-106",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Growth happens in the discomfort. Sometimes productive, meaningful conversations are uncomfortable. Just because something is uncomfortable does not mean it is unhealthy or unsafe. We aim to hold space for negative feelings and confusion. Instead of ignoring these feelings, we encourage you instead to ask questions and voice your concerns. We build trust through honesty, patience, and open communication. (Values: Community, Radical Love, Relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-107",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Pay attention to patterns of participation and power dynamics in this space. We recognize that there are many factors that impact interaction. Remain attentive to patterns of participation in the community and your participation within the collective dynamic. Acknowledge and name who is speaking (role within organization, personal relationships, social identities, race, financial context, ability, etc) as well as who the audience is. Don’t wait for the facilitator."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-108",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Be aware of the context in which an interaction is occurring (place, audience, time, etc). Be aware of yourself and self-monitor the frequency and content of your contributions. This is a community responsibility. (Values: Community, relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-109",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Acknowledge and be accountable for your impact. We recognize that it is not our place to determine whether or not we have caused harm, only to own the impact that we have had. Harm happens on a spectrum. Be active and courageous in the restorative process–if you feel that you have harmed, or someone has communicated that you caused harm, take steps to repair it. Repairing harm can be a collaborative process and can include managers and other team members when support is needed. (Values: community, dignity, relationships)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-110",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Take steps to communicate harm or agree to move past the conflict accepting it as part of your own work (72 hr.): We recognize that in order to further accept and normalize conflict as part of transformative work, we need to reflect, confront, and heal. This work will trigger emotions (such as pain or confusion and social power dynamics). Once harm is realized, go directly to the person who may have harmed you or whom you are concerned you may have harmed looking to gain clarity as well as move forward."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-111",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This does not mean reaching resolution in 72 hrs. This means taking a step towards reflection, conversation or healing. This will look different in every situation and to every individual. There is no one way to move forward. (Values: relationships, dignity)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-112",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Notes:** New Page"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-113",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "## Our People"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-114",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Change/Add:** Add Norms:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-115",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "NewRoot Internal Norms"
        }
      ]
    },
